title: fetch_max_bytes truncates only after the whole body was downloaded and decoded
updated: 2026-10-15T04:48:08Z
---

`_fetch_one` requests the complete response and cuts the decoded body afterwards (`lib/Net/Async/WebSearch.pm:159-170`; the comment there says so). The POD presents `fetch_max_bytes` as 'truncate the response body to this many bytes' (`WebSearch.pm:759`). With `fetch => N` the URLs come from search results, i.e. from arbitrary third parties, at up to `fetch_concurrency` (default 100) in parallel: a result pointing at a multi-gigabyte download or an endless stream is buffered in full, and without `fetch_timeout` it never ends. The limit also counts characters after charset decoding, not bytes.

Done when: the fetch aborts the transfer once the limit is reached (Net::Async::HTTP `on_header`/body-chunk callback, or a `Range` request as a first step), a sane default `fetch_timeout` and `fetch_max_bytes` exist, and the POD states what is bounded.

